00:00:00.110 --> 00:00:07.540 align:start position:0%
 
[Music]  I'm Anthony mangieri of Hoonah Pete  Sinopoli Tanana Lower East Side New York  City well the concept of this restaurant  is really inspired by the pizza of  Naples but kind of like my own twist on  that for me Neapolitan pizza is what I  make because my family is from there and  what I would say is that it's cooked in  a wood-fired oven it's round it's about  12 inches it's served whole it's not  sliced so I don't know if we're making  here is exactly classified like it's  Neapolitan pizza well if you're making  it the way that we do here the dough is  not refrigerated and it's naturally  leavened so you're dealing with like an  element that's slightly under control  and slightly out of control and then you  add in the factor of the oven which  there's no real accurate temperature  controls so I think Neapolitan pizza at  its best and when it's really magical is  when it's riding that line between  perfection and disaster  so the way that we make the pizza here  is the dough is natural 11 so there's no  commercial use in it and it's never  refrigerated it takes two days for it to  raise from beginning to end it's a very  wet though it's super highly hydrated  and what that allows me to do is create  a dough that's very puffy around the  crust and kind of wild I take the dough  out of the tray and I flatten it very  gently very quickly try to leave as much  air in it as possible so that has  character and life then I put the tomato  sauce on it which is the same as oh no  tomatoes from around Naples then I put  the buffalo mozzarella on it which we  cut in cubes one of the big things with  the mozzarella for me is the way that it  melts American mozzarella when it melts  usually dries flatter on the pizza gets  little holes in it it'll get like little  brown spots it's beautiful but it just  not for what I'm looking for then I use  for Sicilian sea salt and drop any fresh  basil which is locally grown if possible  the Sorrento olive oil I want everything  on the pizza to be balanced and even I  don't want one ingredient to be over  another they're kind of working together  as one and then we slide it on to the  pizza peel and then it goes into the  oven once it's in the oven I use little  pieces of wood shavings which is a very  old technique from Naples what the wood  shavings do is they give a little burst  of temperature and it's a temperature  that's just air heat it's not actually  changing the stone temperature the oven  is from Naples it's called the McCune  toe and the design on them are exactly  the same as the design on the ovens in  Pompeii and it just creates this perfect  circulation of heat and it cooks for  like a minute to a minute and a half  take it out and that's  [Music]  my family is from Naples and I fell in  love with Naples when I was a young kid  and will go there and eat that pizza it  just tasted so ethnic it's like so not  American and kind of became evident that  this is really where my heart is and  where I belong so I started and I opened  the first tuna pizza in 1996 for me at  this point of my life like I don't think  that the pizzas the best I think that  it's just mine and it's what I offer so  people come in I hope that they  appreciate that the pizza that you get  here is kind of unique to this place  and to me into everyone here as we work  as a team so that's what I bring to New  York I've been myself and my ideas and  my independence in the way that I do  things  [Music]

